=== 23:22 UTC 2026-09-12 PRE-REGISTRATION -- ARM D: does the bench measure the turn driver production runs?
Finding that motivates this (determinism review, inventory pass): src/zakcode/agent/loop.py carries TWO turn drivers --
`_run_turn` (buffered, ~1,376 lines) behind the sync `run_turn()` the bench calls (bench/run_task.py:426), and
`astream_turn` (streaming, ~1,738 lines) that the CLI, the server and the client use -- i.e. what the coach runs. A
static census finds the same 36 intervention kinds in both, but the streaming driver calls helpers the buffered one
does not (`_prompt_cache_key`, `_rejection_retry_temperature`, `_retry_delay`, `_busy_lease`, `_anchor_prompt`,
`_execute_tool_call` vs `_call_provider`/`_execute_batch`). Every bench number in ADR-0147..0162 was produced by the
buffered driver. Whether those numbers transfer to production is therefore an EMPIRICAL question, and a cheap one.
Design: add ZBENCH_DRIVER=stream to bench/run_task.py -- when set, the task is driven through `astream_turn` (the
production driver) and the same report fields are derived from the final event; default stays `run_turn`. Same build
(main @ b0ee395), same env as the H2H/ARMC cells (35B, temp 0, identity, pinned workspace, no catalogue), N=3.
Tasks: m01-m05, 06-plugin-conventions (its ARMC cell = run_turn reference), 02-median-bug (ADR-0157 reference cell
re-run on run_turn tonight so both drivers are measured on the same build).
Rules:
  D1  every task byte-identical across drivers (stream cell digests == run_turn cell digests, all runs) AND same
      verify outcomes AND same turn counts -> the bench measures production's driver; prior ADRs transfer as-is.
  D2  any task whose bytes differ across drivers -> named, with the first differing file; outcomes compared; a
      differing task with the SAME outcome is a determinism finding (two code paths, two basins), a differing
      OUTCOME is an instrument-validity finding that goes into the review as its first item.
  D3  a stream cell that cannot produce a report (no final event, exception) is an instrument refusal, not a result.
Prediction: D1 holds on m01, m03, m04, m05 (short, 3-4 turns); 06 and 02 and m02 are where a helper-path
difference (retry temperature, cache key timing) could show, so no prediction on those beyond "outcomes hold".

=== 23:37 UTC 2026-09-12 ARM D ADDENDUM (registered while the chain runs; the control cells start only after ARMD COMPLETE)
Observed so far: stream cells match the buffered ones in outcome and turn count (m01 4/4/4, m02 7/7/7, m03 4/4/4) but
take 3-5x the wall time (m03: 57/70/97 s stream vs 16/18/33 s buffered at 23:05). Two explanations, not separable
from the chain's own timing because the buffered references ran 30 min earlier: (a) pod load rose (the coach is live);
(b) the streaming driver is slower per turn on this box (per-token event handling on a 2 GB box, or the SSE path).
Control (queued as /root/zb-armd-lat.sh, runs after the ARMD marker): m03 buffered N=3, then m03 stream N=3, then m03
buffered N=3 again, back to back on the same pod minute. Rules:
  DL1  median stream / median(buffered before, buffered after) >= 2.0 with the two buffered cells within 1.5x of each
       other -> the DRIVER is slower; goes into the review as F1's latency half (production pays it; the bench never saw it).
  DL2  the buffered cells are themselves >= 2x slower than at 23:05 -> pod load; the stream numbers above are not the
       driver's. DL3  neither -> inconclusive at N=3; report.
Prediction: none -- both mechanisms are plausible.

